<Fragment slot="heading">The philosophy degree I didn’t know I <span style="font-style:italic">needed</span></Fragment>
<p style="margin:0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">For years I’d go a bit quiet when someone asked what I studied. Philosophy sounded like the long way round to a career in payments and AI, and I half believed that myself. It took me a while to notice that I lean on it most days, just not in a way anyone would see from the outside.</p>
<p style="margin:22px 0 0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">Nobody in a planning meeting wants to hear about Kant, and they’re right. What stuck with me from college wasn’t the names. It was how you got marked. You could write a clever essay defending almost anything, but you lost marks if you knocked down a weak version of the other side. The good essays found the strongest objection going, stated it fairly and still made their case.</p>
<div style="margin-top:34px;font-size:17px;font-weight:600">Where it shows up</div>
<p style="margin:9px 0 0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">Prioritising is mostly choosing between two good things. People rarely ask for something daft. The team that wants the reporting fix has real customers chasing them. The team pushing the new agent feature has a real window that might close. When I pick one, someone I like and respect goes back to their desk and has to tell their own people why their thing isn’t happening.</p>
<p style="margin:22px 0 0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">That’s the part of the job I think we undersell. Anyone can rank a backlog. The harder skill is describing the other option so well that the person who wanted it nods along before you explain why you went the other way. If I can’t do that, I usually haven’t understood the decision yet, and there’s a fair chance I’m making it for the wrong reason.</p>
<div style="margin-top:34px;font-family:Newsreader,serif;font-size:25px;line-height:1.25;max-width:52ch">"If I can’t make the case for the thing I’m saying no to, I’m not ready to say no."</div>
<div style="margin-top:34px;font-size:17px;font-weight:600">Where I get it wrong</div>
<p style="margin:9px 0 0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">I don’t always manage it. Under pressure I reach for a scoring matrix, because a number feels kinder than a judgement call. I’m not sure it is. People can tell when a spreadsheet is doing the talking so that nobody has to own the decision. The calls that matter most come down to what we think is more important right now. I’d rather say that out loud and take the disagreement than hide it behind a weighted score.</p>
<div style="margin-top:26px;border:1px dashed var(--dash)"><div data-shot="1" style="height:190px;background:var(--stripe);display:flex;align-items:flex-end;padding:20px"><span style="font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace;font-size:10.5px;color:var(--muted);letter-spacing:.08em">[ OPTIONAL DIAGRAM ]</span></div></div>
<div style="margin-top:34px;font-size:17px;font-weight:600">The nugget</div>
<p style="margin:9px 0 0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">Next time you’re about to deprioritise something, first write a few sentences making the best case for it, as if you were the person who asked for it. Send them along with your decision. If you can’t write them, wait a day before you decide.</p>
</BlogPost>
